<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n103857" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τήνελλα</span></b> , a word formed by <i><span class="en">
			Archil.
		</span></i> (<i><span class="en">
			<u>Fr.</u>
			119
		</span></i>) to imitate the twang of a guitar-string (cf. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">θρεττανελό</span>): he began a triumphal hymn to
		Heracles with <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τήνελλα, ὦ καλλίνικε
		χαῖρε</span>; hence the words <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τήνελλα
			καλλίνικε</span> became a common mode of saluting conquerors in
		the games, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>Hurrah!</i>, <i><span class="en">
				Ar.
				<u>Av.</u>
				1764
			</span></i> (lyr.), <i><span class="en">
				<u>Ach.</u>
				1227
			</span></i> (cf. Sch.<i><span class="en">
				Pi.
				<u>O.</u>
				9.1
			</span></i>):— hence </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>II.</b>
			Adj.
			<b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τήνελλος</span></b> , <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ον</span>, <i>greeted with huzzas</i>, 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἐὰν . . νικᾷς . . , τήνελλος εἶ</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Ar.
					<u>Eq.</u>
					276
				</span></i>
			. (Written <b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τήνεβλα</span></b> 
			and <b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τήνεβλος</span></b>  in <i><span class="en">
				Hsch.
			</span></i>)</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
